Surface delta inside `primary-persona/`:

  - `primary-persona/src/contract.py` — `PersonaContract` extended
    with `dev_intent: Literal["unanswered", "yes", "no"]` field
    (default `"unanswered"`). Per locked owner ruling D-MASTER.1
    (a) the answer lives on the contract itself; the Literal
    constraint structurally rejects any other string at validation
    time (AC.A.2 / AC.PO.1).

  - `primary-persona/src/onboarding.py` — `ONBOARDING_QUESTIONS`
    canonical tuple grows from three to four entries; the new
    entry has id `"dev_intent"`, `required=True`, and
    `contract_field="dev_intent"`. The starter-pending
    contributor's body derives the question count from
    `len(ONBOARDING_QUESTIONS)` per D-A.3 (no hard-coded count;
    future fifth question won't drift the prose). Two new helpers
    — `dev_intent_storage_path(workspace_root)` resolver (AC.A.5)
    and `read_dev_intent(workspace_root) -> Literal["yes","no",
    "absent"]` reader (AC.A.6). The `persist_elicitation_transcript`
    write-back path normalises a free-text yes/no answer to the
    contract's Literal admissible values (synonyms accepted:
    yes/y/develop/dev/pos-v2/true → `"yes"`; no/n/use/user/
    harness/false → `"no"`); an unrecognised non-empty answer
    raises `OnboardingTranscriptError` (structural defect, not
    incomplete-transcript).

  - `primary-persona/src/observability.py` — two new event helpers:
    `onboarding_dev_intent_question_event` (fires once per starter
    session at question time) and `onboarding_dev_intent_answer_event`
    (fires once when the answer is recorded; carries the
    normalised literal, not the user's free-text). Both emit
    under `pos.persona.onboarding.dev_intent_*` so observability
    consumers can count them without filtering on the generic
    `question_id` attribute (AC.A.7).

Behaviour:

  - `is_starter` lifecycle is unchanged in shape: the four-answer
    complete transcript flips it to False; an incomplete
    transcript leaves it True. The new entry's `required=True`
    means a missing dev_intent answer counts as "incomplete" and
    prevents the flip — sub-plan A consumers (E / B / F) treat
    `dev_intent="unanswered"` as "no" via sub-plan E's `"absent"`
    mapping; locked owner ruling D-MASTER.4.
  - The reader funnels through the persona contract's existing
    `load_contract` surface; a malformed contract returns
    `"absent"` (fail-safe — the reader never raises). A workspace
    with no `personas/` directory returns `"absent"`. The reader
    prefers a primary-flagged contract; otherwise alphabetical
    first.
  - The persona-template prompt prose is the workspace's call per
    D-MASTER.3; the framework holds the scaffolding (id +
    required + contract_field). The framework ships a starter
    prompt; workspaces override via the existing
    template-override mechanic (amendment #36).
  - Sub-plan A's `dev_intent_storage_path` is the resolver-as-API
    seam: sub-plans E / B / F consume it, not the contract layout
    directly, so D-MASTER.1's choice between (a) and (b) can move
    without breaking downstream (asymmetric observation #2).

