Method-level choices (builder's call per ODD §1.1):

  - D-build.1 (Literal vs Enum): `Literal["unanswered","yes","no"]`.
    Pydantic Literal is the lighter-weight reach-for than StrEnum
    for a closed set of three string values; ODD §5.3 reach-for
    (Pydantic + field_validator) is the default. Already in the
    WIP diff carried through halt-and-resume.
  - D-build.2 (synonym normalisation set): `{yes, y, develop,
    dev, pos-v2, true}` → `"yes"`; `{no, n, use, user, harness,
    false}` → `"no"`. Case-insensitive, whitespace-trimmed. The
    contract's Literal typing structurally constrains the
    admissible set; the normaliser is the sanitiser between
    free-text and validation.
  - D-build.3 (event-name shape): `pos.persona.onboarding.dev_intent_question`
    + `pos.persona.onboarding.dev_intent_answer`. Mirrors the
    existing `pos.persona.onboarding.<event>` namespace. Distinct
    event types (not generic question/answer with a question_id
    attribute) so consumers can count dev-intent prompts without
    attribute filtering.
  - D-build.4 (storage-path resolver shape): the resolver returns
    `<workspace_root>/personas` (the directory the reader walks
    to find the primary persona's contract.yaml). Locked owner
    ruling D-MASTER.1 (a): the answer is on the persona contract
    itself; the path-resolver hides the walk so consumers compose
    on a stable seam.
  - D-build.5 (incomplete-transcript-keeps-unanswered): when the
    transcript omits dev_intent, the contract field stays at
    `"unanswered"` (its prior value) — best-effort partial
    application from amendment #35 carries forward. The contract
    is re-written with the same unanswered value; the reader
    returns `"absent"` (per AC.A.6's mapping). is_starter stays
    True.

